// TASK 2: CA Hierarchy
$task_contents[2] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Why Certificates Are Never Issued Directly by the Root</h2>

    <p>A Certificate Authority, or CA, is an entity trusted to vouch for identities by issuing digital certificates. In any real PKI deployment, certificates are not issued directly by a single all powerful CA. Instead, trust flows down through a hierarchy: a root CA, one or more intermediate CAs, and issuing CAs that handle day to day certificate requests.</p>

    <p>Think of the root CA as a master authority kept locked away in a vault, brought out only to authorize regional offices. Those regional offices, the intermediate and issuing CAs, do the actual daily work of signing certificates for servers and users. If a regional office is ever compromised, the vault itself remains untouched and can be used to revoke that office's authority.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Why This Matters</h4>
        <p>Keeping the root CA offline and rarely used limits the blast radius of a compromise. If an issuing CA is breached, only its certificates need to be revoked and reissued under a new intermediate. The root itself stays clean.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Root vs Intermediate vs Issuing CA</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>CA Type</th>
                <th>Typical State</th>
                <th>Exposure Risk</th>
                <th>What It Signs</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Root CA</strong></td>
                <td>Offline, kept in cold storage</td>
                <td>Very low, rarely used</td>
                <td>Intermediate CA certificates only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Intermediate CA</strong></td>
                <td>Online, used periodically</td>
                <td>Moderate</td>
