// TASK 4: Revocation: CRL and OCSP
$task_contents[4] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Trusting a Certificate Is Not Permanent</h2>

    <p>A certificate can become untrustworthy before it expires. A private key might be compromised, an employee might leave the organization, or a certificate might have been mis-issued. When that happens, the certificate must be revoked so relying parties stop trusting it immediately, not just when its expiry date finally arrives.</p>

    <p>There are two main ways to check whether a certificate has been revoked. A Certificate Revocation List, or CRL, is a signed list of revoked certificate serial numbers published periodically by the CA. The Online Certificate Status Protocol, or OCSP, allows a relying party to query the status of a single certificate in real time instead of downloading an entire list.</p>

    <h3>CRL vs OCSP</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Mechanism</th>
                <th>Check Type</th>
                <th>Latency</th>
                <th>Scalability Concern</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>CRL</strong></td>
                <td>Full list, downloaded periodically</td>
                <td>Can be stale between publications</td>
                <td>List grows large over time</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>OCSP</strong></td>
                <td>Single certificate, real time query</td>
                <td>Near real time</td>
                <td>Responder load scales with traffic</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <!-- SVG: OCSP request/response flow
         Teaching purpose: show OCSP as a live per-certificate query,
         which they would otherwise confuse with the CRL's downloaded-list model. -->
    <div style="margin: 20px 0;">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 700 140" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="OCSP request and response flow">
            <rect x="20"  y="40" width="160" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="100" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="var(--text-primary)">Client</text>
            <rect x="270" y="40" width="180" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="360" y="65" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="var(--text-primary)">OCSP Responder</text>
            <text x="360" y="83" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--text-secondary)">Checks cert status live</text>
            <rect x="530" y="40" width="150" height="60" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="605" y="65" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="var(--text-primary)">Good / Revoked</text>
            <text x="605" y="83" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--text-secondary)">/ Unknown</text>
            <line x1="180" y1="70" x2="265" y2="70" stroke="var(--text-secondary)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr4)"/>
            <line x1="450" y1="70" x2="525" y2="70" stroke="var(--text-secondary)" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#arr4)"/>
            <defs>
                <marker id="arr4" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="3" orient="auto">
                    <path d="M0,0 L0,6 L8,3 z" fill="var(--text-secondary)"/>
                </marker>
            </defs>
        </svg>
    </div>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Why This Matters</h4>
        <p>OCSP stapling lets a server attach a timestamped, signed OCSP response directly to its certificate during the TLS handshake, so the client does not need to contact the responder itself. This improves both speed and privacy. If neither CRL nor OCSP checking is configured at all, a revoked or stolen certificate will still be trusted, which is exactly the kind of gap attackers rely on.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

// TASK 5: Certificate Lifecycle Management
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>A Certificate Is Never a One Time Event</h2>

    <p>Issuing a certificate is only the beginning. Every certificate moves through a lifecycle: it is requested, issued, deployed to the system that will use it, monitored while active, and eventually renewed or revoked and retired. Neglecting any stage of this cycle creates real operational and security risk.</p>

    <p>Automation tools such as ACME, the Automatic Certificate Management Environment protocol, have made it far easier to handle renewal at scale, reducing the chance that a certificate silently expires and causes an outage.</p>

    <!-- SVG: lifecycle diagram
         Teaching purpose: show the lifecycle as an ongoing cycle,
         which they would otherwise treat as a single one-time issuance event. -->
    <div style="margin: 20px 0;">
