<div class="content-block">
    <p>You now understand how PKI establishes trust at scale. Root, intermediate, and issuing CAs delegate authority in layers rather than issuing everything from a single point. Certificate templates and auto-enrolment make issuance consistent and scalable, while CRL and OCSP give relying parties two different ways to check whether a certificate should still be trusted.</p>

    <p>You also saw how certificate lifecycle management keeps a PKI healthy over time, and how weak keys, excessive validity periods, and missing revocation checking combine to create real impersonation and MITM risk. These are the exact details that separate a well engineered PKI from one waiting to be exploited.</p>

    <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Trust flows through a hierarchy: root CA, intermediate CA, issuing CA, then end entity certificates.</li>
        <li>Certificate templates define policy, and auto-enrolment applies that policy automatically at scale.</li>
        <li>CRL is a periodically published list, while OCSP checks a single certificate's status in real time.</li>
        <li>Certificate lifecycle management is an ongoing cycle, not a one time issuance event.</li>
        <li>Weak keys, excessive validity, and missing revocation checking combine to enable impersonation and MITM attacks.</li>
